 Status: shadow-only Library Truth analysis; no live-library adoption or file changes.
 
 ## Purpose
 
-Alpha4 hardens the recording layer discovered by the first full 7,169-file alpha3 desktop run. It keeps the Broadcast → Recording → Physical File model, but refuses to call a recording family trustworthy when filename lineage, multipart numbering, duration families, or same-date slot coverage contradict it.
+Alpha5 is the focused correction pass derived from the complete alpha4 desktop export. It preserves alpha4’s conservative source and variant boundaries while preventing harmless multipart suffixes from fragmenting one recording family.
 
-## Alpha4 changes
+## Alpha5 changes
 
-- V1/V2 and source filename families are isolated before multipart assembly.
-- Bare trailing numbers are no longer treated as parts per file; a contiguous sibling 1..N sequence is required.
-- Leading track numbers before named dates remain track numbers.
-- A single numbered segment with no declared total is reported as unknown extent, never “1 of 1 incomplete”.
-- Repeated duration clusters plus programme-specific filename evidence trigger a suspicious-merge review.
-- Standard recordings matching a sibling slot or the combined AM+PM duration are flagged for coverage modelling.
-- Adoption readiness is downgraded whenever these recording-structure risks remain.
+- Attached date suffixes such as `10-31-2002a/b` now share one multipart family.
+- Segment quality/state suffixes such as `-partial` and `-ph` no longer create separate recording lineages.
+- Programme evidence such as FNR and Eddie Trunk remains meaningful and is not stripped.
+- Context-confirmed bare `1..N` sequences now persist their effective part number and total into file-level shadow rows and exports.
+- New regressions cover the three alpha4 desktop family splits and the promoted-part display inconsistency.
 
 ## Safety
 
